      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the probability that two randomly chosen members from a club of 10 members (5 boys and 5 girls) are both girls. Let's break down the steps to find the solution.\n\n### Step 1: Determine the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of ways to choose 2 members from 10. This can be done using the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} \\), which represents the number of ways to choose \\( k \\) elements from a set of \\( n \\) elements without regard to the order of selection.\n\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!(10-2)!} = \\frac{10!}{2! \\cdot 8!}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the factorials:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = \\frac{90}{2} = 45\n\\]\n\nSo, there are 45 ways to choose 2 members out of 10.\n\n### Step 2: Determine the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 5\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways to choose 2 girls from the 5 girls available. Using the combination formula again:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2! \\cdot 3!}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the factorials:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5 \\times 4 \\times 3!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = \\frac{20}{2} = 10\n\\]\n\nSo, there are 10 ways to choose 2 girls out of 5.\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the probability\n\nThe probability that both chosen members are girls is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes (choosing 2 girls) to the total number of outcomes (choosing any 2 members from 10).\n\n\\[\n\\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Number of ways to choose 2 girls}}{\\text{Total number of ways to choose 2 members}} = \\frac{10}{45}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{45} = \\frac{2}{9}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that both chosen members are girls is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{9}}\n\\]",
