      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the probability that two randomly chosen members from a group of 10 (5 boys and 5 girls) are both girls.\n\n**Step-by-step reasoning:**\n\n1. **Total number of ways to choose 2 members from 10:**\n   The total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10 is given by the combination formula:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!(10-2)!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n   \\]\n\n2. **Number of ways to choose 2 girls from 5 girls:**\n   The number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 5 is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n   \\]\n\n3. **Probability that both chosen members are girls:**\n   The probability is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes (choosing 2 girls) to the total number of outcomes (choosing any 2 members):\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{both girls}) = \\frac{\\text{Number of ways to choose 2 girls}}{\\text{Total number of ways to choose 2 members}} = \\frac{10}{45} = \\frac{2}{9}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that the two chosen members are both girls is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{9}}\n\\]",
